"Doug Freyburger" <dfreybur@yahoo.com> writes:
> > [ Cheating includes: ]
> > o- using tools which automate non-trivial portions of the game play
> >    (as opposed to just making the interface nicer)
> 
> Thus you consider the pudding farming bot to be cheating?

There are differences of degree. Yes, I do, but I see it as far less
severe than, say, hacking the source to make /oWishing a hundred times
more common.

I also consider the P-farming bot to have considerable technical
merit, and the problem of writing nethack bots in general to be
fascinating.  I respect the author and see the bot as a positive
advancement of the state of the nethacking arts.

I certainly wouldn't post YAAP if I'd used such a thing, or submit a
bones file to Hearse, or feel any sense of accomplishment were I to
ascend.

> I do think the process should be fixed by having pudding splits
> count against their extinction limit.

That makes that particular bot pointless; it does not address the
issue of bots (and fairness thereof) in general. Talking about
"fixing" P-farming is more than a bit disingenouous when there's
ongoing controversy over whether it's actually broken in the first
place.

Assuming for the sake of argument that it needs to be nerfed, making
splits respect the extinction limit is one of the saner proposals I've
heard for so doing.


